shift among Ohio's Republican-leaning voters could tighten the race in the final weeks.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Jon Husted (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Ohio's special Senate race pits appointed Republican incumbent Jon Husted against former Senator Sherrod Brown in a state that leans Republican but has shown a favorable environment for Democrats this cycle. Recent polling from mid-to-late September uniformly shows Brown ahead, with margins ranging from a dead heat to eight points, including surveys from Republican-leaning pollsters. Brown also holds a substantial financial advantage, having raised roughly $38.6 million to Husted's $14.4 million, though outside groups have spent more against Brown.\n\nExpert raters mostly call the race a Tossup, with a few rating it Lean D, and prediction markets put Brown's chances near 60 percent. Husted retains the benefits of incumbency and a Republican-leaning electorate, and a Trump rally is planned in his behalf. The forecast lands on a modest Democratic edge, with meaningful uncertainty given Ohio's conservative tilt and the possibility of late movement.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.7221, "margin": 2.94}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.7246, "margin": 3.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.6986, "margin": 2.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.7024, "margin": 2.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.6349, "margin": 2.5}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.6393, "margin": 2.5}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.6851, "margin": 2.5}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.7568, "margin": 3.5}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.7432, "margin": 3.5}]}